.TH PRINT 2
.SH NAME
print, fprint, bio_print \- formatted output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libfmt"
.PP
.nf
.B "print :: proc(format: string, fargs: ..any) -> i64"
.B "fprint :: proc(fd: int, format: string, fargs: ..any) -> i64"
.B "bio_print :: proc(b: ^libuser.Bio, format: string, fargs: ..any) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Print
formats
.I format
and
.I fargs
through
.BR core:fmt 's
buffer formatting and writes the result to descriptor 1 in one call.
.I Fprint
does the same to the descriptor
.IR fd .
Both answer what
.IR read (2)
answered.
.I Bio_print
formats into a
.IR bio (2)
buffer rather than writing directly, so a tool that prints a piece at a
time still makes one system call per buffer.
.PP
Formatting goes through a 1024-byte stack buffer.
A line longer than that is cut to fit; nothing is allocated.
.PP
This package exists apart from
.I libuser
because importing
.B core:fmt
pulls in the Odin runtime's 128-bit arithmetic helpers, which cost
kilobytes no small program wants to pay.
A tool that formats imports
.BR libfmt ;
a program that fits a page does not.
.SH EXAMPLES
A server answers a
.B Tversion
by formatting nothing at all, because
.I libfmt
is for tools, not servers.
A tool reports a count:
.IP
.EX
libfmt.print("%d lines\en", count)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libfmt/print.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bio (2),
.IR read (2),
.IR errstr (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A negative return is the write's error, from
.IR read (2).
.SH BUGS
A message longer than 1024 bytes is silently cut.
There is no
.I sprint
or
.I smprint
that allocates a result string.
